@namespace NSail.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing
@implements IDisposable

@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject RootSurface Root

@* A link whose destination only redraws a surface over the page the user is already on is
   ours to drive: Blazor's own link interception pushes every anchor it follows, and only one
   of the three surface transitions is a push (SurfaceContext.Follow) — moving the page inside
   an open surface replaces, and so does an open this link marked NoHistory. Preventing the
   click's default is what stands that interception down — it skips a click already
   defaultPrevented. Even where the answer agrees with Blazor's, the click has to come through
   here: an open is what records itself as pushed, and the close that pops reads that.
   It is done from a wrapper rather than on the anchor because two of the three chromes render
   their anchor inside a vendor component that exposes no way to prevent anything, and a click
   prevented anywhere on its way up is prevented for the whole event. The anchor keeps its href
   either way, so the address stays copyable and deep-linkable; only how the click stacks
   changes.
   A link that leaves the page renders no wrapper at all — a bare anchor Blazor pushes, with
   no handler to dispatch to the server on every ordinary click in the app.
   The prevention itself is not written here: a markup `@onclick:preventDefault` is decided
   when the element renders, and whether a click deserves preventing is a property of the
   CLICK — ctrl+click and middle-click are the user asking the browser for the address, which
   is why Blazor's own interceptor lets them through. The class this wrapper carries is the
   hook a document listener answers on, per click, on those same terms (ns.js); Follow reads
   the same flags below so the two ends cannot disagree.
   Stopping propagation too (not just the default) is what keeps this link honest wherever it
   renders: a grid row with its own OnRowClick (the counter door's Clientes grid) would
   otherwise see the same click a beat after Follow already fired, and NavigationManager's
   second call wins — the row's own destination silently replaces whatever the link just
   opened. It is unconditional, modifier click included: a new tab opening is no reason for
   the row underneath to navigate too. NsActionToolbar already carries this exact guard for
   its own wrapper; a bare NsLink used straight in a row template needs it just as much, so it
   belongs here instead of being rediscovered at every call site. *@
@* Two of the three chromes below are vendor buttons, and MudBaseButton reads a cascade named
   ParentDisabled for itself — the same name NsForm cascades. Unshadowed, a link under a form
   that is merely SAVING goes dark for the length of the save by the vendor's rule, with nothing
   NSail decided in it: a link submits nothing and can declare nothing, so a save it has no part
   in is not its business (NsButton carries the same shadow for the same reason).
   Shadowed to false so IsDisabled below is the whole of it — the link owes its caller one rule,
   and the vendor is not a second opinion (principles.md, the vendor stops at the boundary). *@
<CascadingValue Value="false" Name="ParentDisabled" IsFixed="true">
    @if (_intercepts)
    {
        <span class="ns-link-intercept" @onclick="Follow" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">@Chrome</span>
    }
    else
    {
        @Chrome
    }
</CascadingValue>

@code {

    RenderFragment Chrome => @<text>
@* The flat rung has no box: with a word it is a bare anchor (its own Icon riding beside the
   word when there is one), with a glyph and no word it is the vendor's own icon button — the
   very control a grid row's actions wear. Every other intention keeps the chromed button and
   loses its label to ns-square, so a command and a link at one intention read as one button
   (NsButton draws the same two faces).
   The word-and-icon anchor never reads Breakpoint, unlike NsButton's own worded Inline face:
   it sets no aria-label, so the word IS its whole accessible name, and hiding it below a
   container width would leave the anchor nameless rather than iconed (nsail#1726 review,
   actions.md). *@
@if (As == NsAs.Inline && !Wordless)
{
    <a href="@ActiveHref" target="@BrowserTarget" class="@GetAnchorClasses()" data-ns-probe="@Probe">
        @if (Icon is not null)
        {
            <NsIcon Icon="@Icon" Size="@Size" />
        }
        @Label
    </a>
}
else if (As == NsAs.Inline)
{
    @if (Label is not null)
    {
        @* The tooltip is what a sighted pointer user sees; a screen reader needs the same
           sentence as the element's own accessible name — MudTooltip's popover contributes
           no name on its own, the gap NsButton's own icon chrome already closed. *@
        <MudTooltip Text="@Label" @bind-Visible="_whispering">
            <MudIconButton Href="@ActiveHref"
                           Target="@BrowserTarget"
                           Icon="@(Icon?.Markup)"
                           Size="@GetSize()"
                           Color="Color.Inherit"
                           Disabled="@IsDisabled"
                           Class="@Class"
                           aria-label="@Label"
                           data-ns-probe="@Probe"
                           OnClick="Pressed">
                @Content
            </MudIconButton>
        </MudTooltip>
    }
    else
    {
        <MudIconButton Href="@ActiveHref"
                       Target="@BrowserTarget"
                       Icon="@(Icon?.Markup)"
                       Size="@GetSize()"
                       Color="Color.Inherit"
                       Disabled="@IsDisabled"
                       Class="@Class"
                       data-ns-probe="@Probe">
            @Content
        </MudIconButton>
    }
}
else
{
    @* aria-label restates the visible Label so the accessible name survives a container
       rule collapsing the span to display:none (the emphasis-ladder footer, ns-mud.css) —
       hidden content drops out of the accessible-name computation, the same reason a
       RowEditor field with a blank visible Label still carries its own aria-label. A
       literal unmatched attribute like data-ns-probe already above; Blazor merges both
       into MudButton's own capture rather than needing an explicit dictionary here. *@
    <MudButton Href="@ActiveHref"
               Target="@BrowserTarget"
               Variant="Variant.Filled"
               Color="@(As == NsAs.Main ? Color.Primary : Color.Default)"
               Size="@GetSize()"
               StartIcon="@(Icon?.Markup)"
               Disabled="@IsDisabled"
               Class="@GetChromedClasses()"
               aria-label="@Label"
               data-ns-probe="@Probe">
        <span class="@LabelClasses">@(Wordless ? null : Label)</span>
    </MudButton>
}
    </text>;

    string _href = string.Empty;
    bool _intercepts;

    // The vendor's tooltip opens on hover and on focus and closes on neither after a press: the
    // pointer never left, and the click left the control holding focus. NsButton owns the same
    // whisper for the same reason, and this is here because the two faces answer alike by rule
    // (ui/actions.md, IconOnlyStateTests) — not because a screen was caught doing it. Every link
    // measured either leaves the page or re-renders the row it sits in, and both remount the
    // tooltip, which closes it by accident; one that does neither is the case this removes
    // rather than waits for.
    bool _whispering;

    [CascadingParameter]
    SurfaceContext? Cascaded { get; set; }

    // Resolution never asks whether a cascade arrived. A link that cannot see one still
    // renders inside SOME surface — the page underneath, at worst — and the root surface
    // answers for that one exactly as the cascade would, so a target is honoured wherever
    // the link is rendered: inside a vendor portal (a dropdown, a menu, a dialog's own
    // chrome) whose tree is a sibling of the router, or anywhere a host forgot to provide.
    // Silently downgrading the address to the bare route instead would still be a working
    // full-page navigation, and therefore a failure with nothing to see.
    SurfaceContext Surface => Cascaded ?? Root.Surface;

    /// <summary>A plain navigation link, no wiring beyond the parameters below — the typed
    /// alternative for a destination page is NsPageLink, which derives this and Label.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Href { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Where the link opens: one of the app's surfaces (Surfaces.Aside/Modal),
    /// one of the reserved targets (Surfaces.Auto, Surfaces.Main), or the browser
    /// (Surfaces.Blank).</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Surface? Target { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A place INSIDE the destination — the id of the heading a guide chapter is
    /// being opened at. It rides the resolved href rather than Href itself, because a surface
    /// carries its route as a query VALUE and an anchor written in there would be read as part
    /// of the route: a fragment belongs to the document the browser lands on, whichever surface
    /// draws it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Fragment { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Opens the surface without an entry of its own: Back skips it and closing
    /// rewrites the address instead of popping. For an open the user did not navigate to —
    /// a satellite that exists to feed a value back into a form still standing underneath,
    /// which is the lookup's inline create and the empty picker's door. An ordinary link leaves
    /// it alone: a window someone opened is a place, and Back closes it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool NoHistory { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Intention only — Inline (default), Default, Main, Important. Whether the
    /// label is drawn is Breakpoint's, not this one's.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public NsAs As { get; set; } = NsAs.Inline;

    [Parameter]
    public Glyph? Icon { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Renders instead of the glyph on the Inline rank's icon-only face (a person's
    /// avatar rather than a catalog entry) — the vendor icon button already supports
    /// arbitrary content in place of its Icon string. That face only: the bare anchor draws
    /// NsIcon from Icon directly and never reads Content, and neither do the chromed
    /// branches.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Content { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public NsSize Size { get; set; } = NsSize.Md;

    /// <summary>The link's text where there is one to draw, and its tooltip and accessible
    /// name always — set it either way.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Greys the link out and drops its href — for a destination that isn't
    /// reachable yet (a scoped route whose scope hasn't been picked).</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Container width from which the label shows next to the icon; below it the
    /// link is its glyph alone. Always pins the label on, Never takes it away at every width
    /// — the Label stays the name (tooltip and accessible name) and is not drawn. The one
    /// thing that decides whether the text shows, so a rank never has to. No effect without
    /// an Icon (or a Content standing in for one): there would be nothing left to
    /// click.
    /// <para>No effect either on the Inline rank's bare-anchor face once it carries both an
    /// Icon and a Label (nsail#1726): that anchor sets no aria-label of its own, so the word
    /// is its whole accessible name and collapsing it away would leave the anchor nameless
    /// rather than iconed. It stays on screen at every width.</para></summary>
    [Parameter]
    public NsBreakpoint Breakpoint { get; set; } = NsBreakpoint.Sm;

    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>One cascade carrying two answers OR'd together: the form was Disabled outright,
    /// or a submit is running and every field under it is frozen for its length (NsButton reads
    /// the same name for a command, and so does the vendor). Read here only where the two names
    /// below do not reach — see ParentRefuses.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter(Name = "ParentDisabled")]
    bool ParentDisabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The form's refusal on its own — NsForm cascades it beside the OR precisely so an
    /// act can tell it from the freeze. A link drives no submit, so this is the only half of the
    /// form's word it answers.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter(Name = "FormDisabled")]
    bool FormDisabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Cascaded by NsForm while its own submit handler runs — read only to tell the
    /// freeze apart from the refusal.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter(Name = "FormRunning")]
    bool FormRunning { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Navigation.LocationChanged += OnLocationChanged;
        UpdateHref();
    }

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        UpdateHref();
    }

    void OnLocationChanged(object? sender, LocationChangedEventArgs args)
    {
        UpdateHref();
        _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    // No word to draw at any width: either the breakpoint says Never, or no name was handed
    // over to draw in the first place. Content counts as the glyph — a contributor whose
    // action IS its own image passes one instead of an Icon (ActionItem.Content).
    bool Wordless => (Icon is not null || Content is not null) && (Breakpoint == NsBreakpoint.Never || Label is null or "");

    // A control with no label at any width has nothing to COLLAPSE, so it takes none of the
    // breakpoint's hooks: ns-square carries its own whisper ungated (ns-mud.css), and both
    // hooks at once would draw the bubble twice on the same hover.
    string LabelClasses => Wordless ? "ns-button-label" : NsResponsive.LabelClasses(Icon, Breakpoint);

    // The bare anchor is one text node by default; a caller that also gave it an Icon (the
    // ticket header's Perfil, nsail#1726) needs the glyph laid out beside the word rather than
    // wherever the browser's own inline flow drops it — ns-link-iconed is that row (ns-mud.css).
    // Null rather than NsClassBuilder's own "" once there is nothing to add: Blazor omits an
    // attribute whose value is null (Probe below relies on the same rule), so a plain anchor
    // with no Class and no Icon renders no class attribute at all instead of an empty one.
    string? GetAnchorClasses()
    {
        var classes = new NsClassBuilder(Class).Add("ns-link-iconed", Icon is not null).ToString();

        return classes.Length == 0 ? null : classes;
    }

    // The vendor has no soft-accent variant, so Important is painted from a class over its
    // filled one (ns-mud.css), the same one NsButton wears — a link and a command at the same
    // emphasis must not read as two things. ns-square is that file's answer to an intention
    // with no label, and it is written here for the same reason: one rule, two components.
    string GetChromedClasses()
    {
        return new NsClassBuilder(Wordless ? null : NsResponsive.CollapseClass(Icon, Breakpoint))
            .Add("ns-important", As == NsAs.Important)
            .Add("ns-square", Wordless)
            .Add(Class)
            .ToString();
    }

    Size GetSize()
    {
        return Size switch
        {
            NsSize.None or NsSize.Xs or NsSize.Sm => MudBlazor.Size.Small,
            NsSize.Lg or NsSize.Xl or NsSize.Xxl => MudBlazor.Size.Large,
            _ => MudBlazor.Size.Medium
        };
    }

    // A form's word is two words, and a link answers one of them. Disabled means it refuses
    // writes, and the link greys with the command beside it — the emphasis ladder makes those
    // two one button to the eye (GetChromedClasses), so one going quiet alone is a footer
    // disagreeing with itself. That half is FormDisabled, the form's own word, so a refusal a
    // running submit is standing on top of still counts. Merely SAVING is not that: the save
    // refuses the acts that DRIVE it, which a link never does — it has no Submits to declare
    // and nothing to re-enter.
    // The second term is for the far side of a PORTAL: NsMenu carries the OR across the vendor's
    // popover and NsDialogExit carries it into a dialog's title row, and neither of the other two
    // names reaches either place — so there the OR is the only word there is, and FormRunning being
    // false there is what makes reading it the right answer rather than a reconstruction.
    bool ParentRefuses => FormDisabled || (ParentDisabled && !FormRunning);

    // Asked by the two chromes that are vendor buttons; the worded face is a bare anchor with no
    // grey to draw, so a form's refusal would reach it only as a silently dropped href — a link
    // that stops working with nothing on screen saying so, which is worse than the one thing it
    // has to offer. Its own Disabled still drops it (ActiveHref), because a destination that is
    // not reachable yet has no address to hand the browser in the first place.
    bool IsDisabled => Disabled || ParentRefuses;

    // A disabled control keeps the chrome but must not navigate: MudBlazor renders an
    // anchor whenever Href is set, and an anchor ignores the disabled attribute.
    string? ActiveHref => Disabled ? null : _href;

    string? BrowserTarget => Target?.IsBrowser == true ? Target.Value.Name : null;

    string Anchored(string href)
    {
        return string.IsNullOrEmpty(Fragment) ? href : $"{href}#{Fragment}";
    }

    void UpdateHref()
    {
        // A destination that carries its own scheme (https:, mailto:, tel:, ...) is outside
        // this app's routes and passes through unchanged; everything else is a route this
        // surface resolves. The question is asked of SurfaceContext.IsExternal and never of
        // Uri directly — the platform trap that cost this story four sessions is named there.
        if (SurfaceContext.IsExternal(Href))
        {
            _href = Anchored(Href);
            _intercepts = false;
            return;
        }

        _href = Anchored(Surface.GetHref(Href, Target));

        // Re-read on every location change (this component already subscribes) because the
        // same link is a surface move or a departure depending on where the user is standing.
        _intercepts = !Disabled
            && BrowserTarget is null
            && Surface.StaysInPlace(_href);
    }

    // A click carrying a modifier, or one made with anything but the primary button, is the
    // user addressing the BROWSER — open in a new tab, in a new window, saved. Its default is
    // left standing (ns.js) and following it in place as well would move the page behind the
    // tab that just opened. The flags asked here are the ones Blazor's own link interception
    // asks, so an intercepted link and an ordinary one answer a modifier the same way.
    void Follow(MouseEventArgs args)
    {
        if (args.Button != 0 || args.CtrlKey || args.ShiftKey || args.AltKey || args.MetaKey)
        {
            return;
        }

        Surface.Follow(_href, Target, NoHistory);
    }

    // Beside the navigation and not instead of it: the vendor wires this onclick whether or not
    // the control carries an href, so taking the bubble down costs the link nothing it does.
    void Pressed()
    {
        _whispering = false;
    }

    // What this link resolved, written where it can be read from either side of the prerender
    // boundary. The output alone cannot say it — a dropped target and a genuine departure both
    // render a bare route — and the console cannot either, because whether the WASM runtime's
    // console reaches a tooling pane is unproven, so an absent line proves nothing. A DOM
    // attribute has no such doubt: whoever rendered the anchor left its own reading on it.
    // "rt" separates the prerender pass from the interactive one, which is the whole question;
    // an anchor carrying NO data-ns-probe at all was not rendered by this component.
    // DEBUG only, and Blazor omits an attribute whose value is null, so RELEASE emits nothing.
#if DEBUG
    string? Probe => string.Join(';',
        $"rt:{(OperatingSystem.IsBrowser() ? "wasm" : "server")}",
        $"raw:{Href}",
        $"target:{Target?.Name ?? "null"}",
        $"cascade:{(Cascaded is null ? "null" : Cascaded.Name?.Name ?? "main")}",
        $"surface:{Surface.Name?.Name ?? "main"}",
        $"href:{_href}",
        $"intercepts:{_intercepts}");
#else
    string? Probe => null;
#endif

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Navigation.LocationChanged -= OnLocationChanged;
    }
}
